    "hypothesis": "I'd be happy to help you prepare for your guitar shopping trip!\n\nWhen looking for a new guitar, here are some key things to consider:\n\n**1. Determine your budget**: Set a realistic budget before you start shopping. Guitars can range from a few hundred to several thousand dollars.\n\n**2. Consider your playing style**: Think about the type of music you want to play and the sound you're looking for. For example:\n\t* If you're into rock or metal, you may want a guitar with humbucking pickups.\n\t* If you're into folk or country, you may prefer a guitar with single-coil pickups.\n\t* If you're a beginner, you may want a guitar with a more forgiving action (string height).\n\n**3. Body style**: Guitars come in various body styles, such as:\n\t* Solid-body (e.g., Stratocaster, Les Paul)\n\t* Semi-hollow body (e.g., ES-335)\n\t* Acoustic (e.g., dreadnought, concert)\n\t* Classical (e.g., nylon-string)\n\n**4. Neck and fingerboard**: Consider the neck width, scale length, and fingerboard material. A comfortable neck is essential for playability.\n\n**5. Pickups and electronics**: If you're looking at electric guitars, think about the type of pickups you want (single-coil, humbucker, or P-90). Also, consider the electronics, such as tone controls and pickup switching.\n\n**6. Action and setup**: Make sure the guitar is set up properly, with a comfortable action and proper intonation.\n\n**7. Playability**: Play the guitar before you buy it! Check for:\n\t* Comfortable neck and body shape\n\t* Easy access to the frets\n\t* Good sustain and tone\n\n**8. Brand and quality**: Research the brand and read reviews to ensure you're getting a quality instrument.\n\n**9. Warranty and support**: Check if the manufacturer offers a warranty and what kind of support they provide.\n\n**10. Try before you buy**: Don't be afraid to try out multiple guitars and ask the sales staff for recommendations.\n\nAdditionally, here are some questions to ask the sales staff:\n\n* What's the guitar's history and condition?\n* Are there any issues with the guitar that I should be aware of?\n* Can I try out different strings or pickups to see how they sound?\n* What's the return policy if I'm not satisfied with the guitar?\n\nRemember, finding the right guitar is a personal preference, so take your time, and don't rush into a purchase. Good luck, and have fun shopping!",
    "autoeval_label": false
}
